# Issue Stage Design Specification

## 1. Purpose

The issue stage is the in-order dispatch and tracking point between instruction decode and instruction execution. It accepts decoded instructions, assigns each instruction a unique identity for tracking, prepares source operands, dispatches instructions to the appropriate execution resource, receives completion information from those resources, and retires completed instructions in program order.

## 2. Reset and Initial State

After reset, the issue stage shall:

- contain no in-flight, unissued, or partially completed instructions;
- have no destination registers reserved by older instructions;
- be ready to accept a new decoded instruction as soon as reset is deasserted;
- not issue, commit, or report completion for any instruction while reset is asserted.

## 3. Instruction Intake

The issue stage shall accept decoded instructions in program order.

An incoming instruction shall be accepted only when:

- the instruction is presented with complete decoded instruction information;
- the issue stage has capacity to track another instruction that has not yet retired;
- the instruction is the next instruction in program order; and
- no full pipeline flush is currently requiring all instruction state to be discarded.

When the issue stage accepts an instruction, it shall:

- acknowledge acceptance;
- assign the instruction a unique transaction identifier;
- record the instruction’s destination register and source operand requirements;
- record the instruction’s program counter, compressed-instruction status, operation, and execution-resource class;
- record any branch-prediction metadata associated with the instruction; and
- make the instruction visible to dependency and forwarding logic so that younger instructions can be ordered correctly.

If the issue stage does not acknowledge acceptance, the incoming instruction shall remain pending and no younger instruction shall be accepted in its place.

## 4. In-Flight Tracking

The issue stage shall track every accepted instruction until that instruction has been retired.

For each tracked instruction, the issue stage shall retain:

- its transaction identifier;
- its program counter and compressed-instruction flag;
- its destination register, if any;
- its source registers and operand requirements;
- the execution resource class it must be sent to;
- its operation selector;
- whether it has been dispatched to execution;
- whether it has completed execution;
- the result data produced by execution, once available;
- any exception information produced by execution; and
- whether it is a control-flow instruction and whether it is still unresolved.

The tracking capacity shall be at least equal to the maximum number of instructions that may be in flight between issue and commit.

## 5. In-Order Issue and Ordering Rules

The issue stage shall dispatch instructions in program order. At most one instruction shall be dispatched to an execution resource in a single cycle.

A younger instruction shall not be dispatched before an older instruction. If the oldest untracked? The oldest tracked instruction that has not yet been dispatched is not ready, then no younger tracked instruction shall be dispatched instead, even if that younger instruction is ready and its execution resource is available.

An instruction shall be considered ready to dispatch only when:

- all required source operand values are available;
- the destination register is not already reserved by an earlier in-flight instruction;
- the target execution resource is ready to accept a new instruction; and
- for a control-flow instruction, no earlier control-flow instruction is still unresolved.

If an instruction is not ready to dispatch, it shall remain in the tracking structure and be reconsidered in later cycles without disturbing the program order.

## 6. Operand Supply

The issue stage shall provide the correct source operand values for every instruction it dispatches.

Source operand values shall be obtained from the architectural register file unless an earlier in-flight instruction is going to write one of the source registers.

If an earlier in-flight instruction will write a source register:

- the issue stage shall use the result of that earlier instruction when that result is available;
- the result may be taken from a completion notification rather than from the architectural register file;
- if the earlier result is not yet available, the instruction shall not be dispatched until the result becomes available.

The supplied operand values shall reflect the most recent earlier instruction that targets each source register, regardless of whether that earlier instruction has been retired to the architectural register file.

The issue stage shall also supply the immediate value required by the instruction, if any.

## 7. Dispatch to Execution Resources

The issue stage shall classify each instruction according to the execution resource it requires. Supported execution resource classes shall include arithmetic/logic, branch, load/store, multiply/divide, and CSR access.

When dispatching an instruction, the issue stage shall provide all information needed by the execution resource, including:

- the selected execution resource class;
- the operation to perform;
- the two source operand values;
- the immediate value, if required;
- the transaction identifier;
- the program counter;
- the compressed-instruction flag; and
- the branch-prediction metadata, if applicable.

The issue stage shall not dispatch an instruction to an execution resource that is not ready to accept it. If the target resource is busy, the instruction shall remain pending and the dispatch attempt shall be repeated later.

## 8. Control-Flow Handling

The issue stage shall allow at most one unresolved control-flow instruction in the machine at any time.

A new control-flow instruction shall not be dispatched until any earlier control-flow instruction has been resolved.

The issue stage shall forward the branch-prediction metadata associated with a dispatched instruction so that the execution resource can determine whether the predicted path was correct.

When a branch resolution notification is received, the issue stage shall update its tracking state so that subsequent control-flow instructions may eventually be dispatched.

## 9. Completion and Write-Back

The issue stage shall accept completion notifications for any in-flight instruction.

Completion notifications shall identify the instruction by its transaction identifier and shall include either a result value or exception information.

The issue stage shall:

- associate the completion with the correct tracked instruction;
- store the result value for that instruction once it is available;
- store any exception information associated with that instruction; and
- preserve the completed instruction for later in-order retirement.

Completions may arrive out of program order. The issue stage shall record each completion independently and shall not require older instructions to complete before younger instructions.

A completion for an instruction that has already been flushed or retired shall be ignored.

Completion of execution shall not by itself update the architectural register file. Architectural state shall be updated only during retirement.

## 10. In-Order Retirement and Architectural Update

The issue stage shall retire instructions in program order.

Only the oldest tracked instruction that has not yet retired shall be presented for retirement. If the oldest instruction has not completed, no younger instruction shall be retired.

When retirement is acknowledged for a completed instruction:

- the instruction shall be removed from in-flight tracking;
- its destination register reservation shall be released; and
- if the instruction has a destination register and no exception, the stored result shall be written to that architectural register;
- if the instruction has an exception, no architectural register update shall be performed.

A write to the architectural zero register shall have no architectural effect.

## 11. Exceptions

If an execution resource reports an exception for an instruction, the issue stage shall retain that exception with the instruction.

An instruction with an exception shall not update architectural state during retirement.

The exception information shall be made available along with the retirement information so that the correct trap handling can be performed by the rest of the core.

## 12. Flush Behavior

The issue stage shall support two forms of flushing:

### 12.1 Flush of Unissued Instructions

When a flush of unissued instructions is requested:

- all accepted instructions that have not yet been dispatched to an execution resource shall be discarded;
- instructions that have already been dispatched shall remain in tracking and shall continue to complete and retire normally;
- the discarded instructions shall never be dispatched, retired, or allowed to update architectural state.

### 12.2 Full Pipeline Flush

When a full pipeline flush is requested:

- all tracked instructions shall be discarded, whether or not they have already been dispatched;
- all destination register reservations shall be cleared;
- all source-operand dependency state shall be cleared;
- any completion notifications that arrive later for flushed instructions shall be ignored;
- no instruction shall be accepted while the flush is active; and
- after the flush, the issue stage shall return to an empty, ready state.
